        <div id="solids-and-carrier" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Active Solids and Carrier: Reading the Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two numbers explain most of the price difference between penetrating sealers. The first is active content, often given as percent solids or percent silane-siloxane. A product with 20 to 40 percent active material carries more repellent into the concrete per gallon than one with a few percent. Commercial bridge-deck silanes can run far higher. Budget products often do not state the figure at all, which is itself a clue.
          </p>

          <p>
            Higher active content generally means deeper penetration and longer service life, but it is not the only factor. Molecule size, carrier, and how well the product is applied all matter. A modest-solids product applied generously in two passes can outperform a high-solids one that was sprayed on lightly and left with dry gaps.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second number is the carrier. Water-borne sealers are low odor, safer to use near living spaces, and clean up with water. Solvent-borne sealers can penetrate dense concrete a little more readily and tolerate cooler application conditions, but they have strong odors, flammability concerns and, in some states, VOC restrictions. For homeowners, water-borne is the sensible default unless the label of a solvent product solves a specific problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concentrates add a third consideration. A jug that dilutes several times with water may look expensive, but the cost per finished gallon can be lower than ready-to-use products. The flip side is that mixing becomes your responsibility. Measure carefully, mix completely, and do not stretch the ratio to cover more ground.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="match-the-substrate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Matching the Sealer to the Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dense, smooth, trowel-finished concrete, such as a garage floor or an interior slab, is hard for any penetrating product to enter. Silane-heavy repellents and lithium densifiers do best here. Before buying, try the water drop test: if water sits on the surface without darkening it after several minutes, the concrete may already be sealed or too dense for a repellent to absorb, and you should investigate before spending money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Broom-finished exterior concrete, the typical driveway and walkway, absorbs readily and suits any good silane-siloxane blend. For driveway-specific picks and salt considerations, see our <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">concrete driveway sealer reviews</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Porous masonry, including brick, split-face block and some natural stone, often does better with siloxane-rich products that bridge larger pores. These surfaces also tend to be vertical, which makes run-down a concern; apply from the bottom up in light passes so the sealer does not sheet off before soaking in. For walls specifically, our <a href="/best-brick-sealer-reviews/">brick sealer guide</a> and <a href="/best-cinder-block-sealer-reviews/">cinder block sealer guide</a> go further.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavers sit between concrete and masonry. Many penetrating sealers are labeled for them, but paver owners often want joint stabilization or color enhancement, which penetrating repellents do not provide. If that is your goal, look at a dedicated <a href="/best-paver-sealer/">paver sealer</a> instead.
          </p>
