        <div id="limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Penetrating Sealers Cannot Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            They do not stop oil. Silane-siloxane repellents are designed around water and dissolved salts. Motor oil and grease are attracted to the same treated surface, so drips still stain, although often more shallowly. A few products advertise added oil repellency, but for a garage floor where stains matter, a coating is the dependable answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            They do not fill cracks or stop running water. A penetrating sealer will reduce dampness through sound concrete, but water entering through a crack, a cold joint or a failed foundation drain needs a crack repair or drainage fix. Treat the source first, then seal the concrete around it.
          </p>

          <p>
            They do not repair damage. Scaling, spalling and pitting are permanent losses of surface material. Sealing a damaged slab slows further deterioration but does not restore the surface. If appearance matters, a resurfacing product may be needed before sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            And they do not change appearance much, which can disappoint people expecting a fresh look. If the concrete is stained, mildewed or faded, clean it first and accept that a penetrating sealer will preserve it as is. For a darker or glossier finish, a film or an enhancing product is required.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Applying a Penetrating Sealer the Right Way
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with clean, dry, bare concrete. Pressure wash to remove dirt and mildew, degrease oil spots, and remove any old film sealer, paint or efflorescence. Then let the slab dry at least a day, longer in cool or shaded areas. Penetrating products cannot soak into pores that are already full of water.
          </p>

          <p>
            A low-pressure pump sprayer is the usual tool. Many labels call for a flood coat: enough product that the surface stays wet for a short time, then a second pass applied wet-on-wet before the first dries. Others call for two separate coats. Follow your product's instructions precisely, because the timing changes how deeply it penetrates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for puddles in low spots. Standing sealer that dries on the surface can leave a shiny or blotchy residue. Back-roll or broom puddles out while the surface is still wet. On vertical surfaces, spray from the bottom up so runs fall onto areas already treated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work within the temperature and weather window on the label, commonly around 40 or 50 to 90 degrees Fahrenheit, with no rain expected for at least half a day to a full day. Keep pets and people off until dry, and protect plants, glass and nearby painted surfaces from overspray. Our <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/">driveway sealing walkthrough</a> covers the full sequence step by step.
          </p>
        </div>

