        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            NaN. How a Penetrating Sealer Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete is full of tiny capillary pores that wick water in from the surface. A film-forming sealer covers those pores with a layer of acrylic, urethane or epoxy. A penetrating sealer goes into them instead. The liquid soaks a few millimeters into the slab, and the active ingredient either lines the pore walls with a water-repellent layer or reacts with the cement chemistry to partly fill and tighten them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because the treatment lives below the surface, there is nothing to wear, peel or delaminate. Tires, shovels and foot traffic contact the concrete itself, not a coating. That is the main reason penetrating sealers dominate on driveways, bridges and parking structures, where film coatings wear out in a few seasons.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water repellents in this group also leave the concrete breathable. Water vapor can still move out through the pores, but liquid water and dissolved salt are pushed back. That matters for slabs on grade and for masonry walls, where trapping moisture behind a film causes blistering, peeling and, in cold climates, freeze damage behind the coating.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is that you see little or nothing. Most penetrating sealers dry clear and matte, with no color enhancement. If you want concrete to look richer or glossier, you need a film, as covered in our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">guide to choosing a concrete sealer</a>, or a penetrating product specifically sold as color-enhancing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="four-chemistries" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            NaN. Silane, Siloxane, Silicate and Siliconate Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Silanes</strong> are the smallest molecules in the family, which lets them reach deeper into dense concrete. They react with moisture in the presence of the slab's alkalinity to form a durable water-repellent lining. High-solids silanes are what transportation agencies typically specify for bridge decks and highway concrete exposed to salt.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Siloxanes</strong> are larger, partly reacted molecules. They do not penetrate dense concrete as deeply, but they bridge the larger pores of brick, block and porous stone and tend to bead water strongly at the surface. Most consumer products blend silane and siloxane to cover both cases. Our <a href="/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/">silane vs. siloxane breakdown</a> explains when the ratio matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Silicates</strong>, in sodium, potassium or lithium form, are densifiers rather than water repellents. They react with calcium hydroxide to form extra binder, hardening the surface and reducing dusting and porosity. <strong>Siliconates</strong> are older water-repellent chemistry, usually potassium or sodium methyl siliconate, often used on porous masonry and as an economical treatment; they react with carbon dioxide in air to form a repellent layer.
          </p>

