          </h2>

          <p>
            Because penetrating sealers are invisible, the water test is your proof. A day or two after curing, sprinkle water across the treated area. Water should bead or sit on the surface without darkening the concrete. Any spot that darkens quickly was missed or under-applied and should get another pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do the test in several places, especially along edges, in low spots, and where you changed sprayer tanks. Gaps tend to appear in predictable spots: the end of each pass, around obstacles, and under overhangs where you could not see well.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Ranges From Labels</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">100-200</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon on broom-finished concrete</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common dry time before vehicle traffic</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5-10 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Repellent service-life claims</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">28 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical wait for new concrete unless label allows sooner</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Over the years, repeat the test each fall. Repellency fades gradually, usually first in tire paths and other high-wear areas. When water starts soaking in within a minute or two, reapply to those areas. You rarely need to redo an entire slab at once.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="layering" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            NaN. Layering, Topcoats and Future Coatings
          </h2>

          <p>
            Densifier then repellent is the most common layering approach, and it works well on old, dusty exterior concrete. Apply the silicate first, let it react for the time stated on the label, then apply the silane-siloxane once the slab is dry. Many manufacturers publish instructions for exactly this combination.
          </p>

          <p>
            A film over a repellent is trickier. Silane-siloxane treatment is designed to keep liquids out, which includes water-based acrylics. Some products allow a topcoat after the repellent has cured or weathered, but adhesion can suffer. If you might want a wet look later, check both labels or start with the film instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan ahead for paint, epoxy and flooring. Silicone-based repellents can interfere with adhesion of paint and coatings, while silicate densifiers usually do not. If a basement floor or garage may get an epoxy, tile or vinyl floor later, use a densifier or a compatible primer system rather than a water repellent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep records. Note the product, date and coverage. Penetrating sealers leave no visible trace, so the next owner, or you in ten years, will have no way to tell what is on the slab without testing. That note makes future decisions easy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a densifier for water problems</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Silicate densifiers harden concrete but repel water only modestly. If your goal is keeping salt and water out of a driveway or wall, you need a silane, siloxane or blend. Use a densifier when the problem is a soft, dusty surface, and pair it with a repellent outdoors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying over an existing film</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A penetrating sealer cannot reach concrete through acrylic, urethane or paint. It will sit on the film, dry to a residue and do nothing. Test with water first; if it beads, find out what is on the slab and strip it before using a penetrating product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing damp concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pores full of water cannot absorb sealer. Applying too soon after washing or rain leaves the treatment shallow and short-lived. Give the slab at least a full dry day, longer in shade or cool weather, and check that the surface color is uniform before starting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving puddles to dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standing sealer in low spots can dry into a shiny, blotchy or sticky patch, especially with high-solids products. Keep a roller or broom handy and work puddles into the surrounding concrete while the surface is still wet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the water test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Because penetrating sealers dry invisible, missed strips are common. A day after application, sprinkle water across the slab and look for areas that darken. Retreat those spots right away rather than finding them after the first winter.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How deep does a penetrating concrete sealer go?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the product, the concrete and the application. Labels and technical sheets commonly describe penetration in the range of a few millimeters, with small-molecule silanes reaching deepest into dense concrete. Porous, older concrete absorbs more. Even shallow penetration provides protection, because the damaging freeze-thaw action happens mainly in the top layer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do penetrating sealers make concrete waterproof?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. They make it water-repellent, which greatly reduces how much water and salt soak in from the surface, but they do not stop water under pressure or water entering through cracks. For a basement with active leaks, address cracks and drainage first. Penetrating sealers are about reducing absorption, not creating a waterproof barrier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a penetrating sealer on pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, many silane-siloxane products are labeled for pavers, and they protect without changing the color. They do not stabilize joint sand or enhance the color, which many paver owners want. If you need those, use a dedicated paver sealer. Always check that the pavers are clean and free of efflorescence before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a penetrating sealer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Premium silane-siloxane repellents are commonly rated for roughly five to ten years on exterior concrete, depending on exposure and active content. Budget repellents usually last less. Silicate densifiers react permanently, but their water-repelling contribution is limited. Use a water drop test each fall rather than relying on the claimed lifespan.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a penetrating sealer stop spalling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can reduce the risk of new spalling and scaling by keeping water and salt out of the surface, which limits freeze-thaw damage. It will not repair spalls that already exist. If spalling is caused by corroding rebar or poor concrete, sealing slows the process but does not stop it entirely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I paint over a penetrating sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the type. Silicate densifiers generally leave concrete receptive to many coatings. Silane and siloxane repellents can interfere with adhesion of paint, epoxy and adhesives because they repel water-based products. If you plan to coat or paint later, check both product labels or choose a densifier instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a penetrating sealer safe indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Water-borne penetrating sealers and silicate densifiers are commonly used indoors and have low odor. Solvent-borne products should be used only with strong ventilation, and some are labeled for exterior use only. Follow the safety data sheet, ventilate well, and keep children and pets away until the treatment is dry.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

